Z||000000||Dan010||12-05-2001||01:45 PM||||Got the ever present puff (getting bigger)of blue smoke after engine has been run and then shut off, short wait, then re-started. Valve guides??<BR> Thinking of removing the heads and getting a valve job done on them. Remove plenum, intake, valve covers then.......<BR>Also, what else would be good to look for at this time. Dont waant do do a complete rebuid<BR>at this time. 86gt 2.8 auto with 90,000.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||12-05-2001||04:33 PM||||(Bad) valve <B>seals</B> can cause the "smoke on startup" problem.  Heads don't have to be removed to do replace them.  ||||reg||3
Z||000002||Dan010||12-05-2001||04:53 PM||||How do you replace the seals with out removing the heads??<BR>How hard??||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||12-05-2001||05:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Dan010:<BR><B>Remove plenum, intake, valve covers then...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Hold the valves up using compressed air (or whatever) w/cyl. you're working on at TDC on its compression stroke.  Compress valve spring (using a valve spring compressor, amazingly enough).  Remove keepers, retainer, valve spring.  The valve seals consist of a "ring" seal in the 2nd groove from top of valve stem, and a "positive" seal at the bottom. <P>Remove valve seals from valve stem - if they're old and crumble off, you'll know you weren't wasting your time here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  Clean valve stem.  Reinstall spring, "positive" valve seal, retainer, compress spring, install little "ring" stem seal.  Install keepers.  Un-compress valve spring.  Move on to next valve... "Next-!"<P>That's just a synopsis, of course.  <BR>  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black86SE||12-06-2001||07:09 PM||||I was having kind of the same problem, although my car would do that after sitting over night. I removed my heads about a month ago so I can do my tranny conversion and paint the engine. I am going to get my valves ground and the seats replaced. The shop I took it to said that it might be a good idea to replace the valve guides and something else also while they are off. That would run me about $250 for both heads. I am also going to replace the main bearing seals and piston rings while I have the engine all tore apart too. My car only has 109,000 miles and I don't think that the pistons really need to be replaced. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Black86SEsig_1.gif">  <BR>Black 1986 SE V6<BR>Automatic - 5-speed soon!<BR>17" Motegi MR7 Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS<BR>AIM ScreenName: Black86se||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dan010||12-06-2001||07:46 PM||||Thanks, will deside which way I shall aprouch<BR>this after I finish a compression check and my front end fixes.(ball joints & tie-rod ends)<BR>Probly will remove heads, so a valve job can be done.<P>Thanks again<BR>Dan||||reg||1
